The expression we got for $\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}$ isn't in terms of only $x$ and $y$ because it includes $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ in it.

Let's plug the original equation $\blueD{\dfrac{dy}{dx}=e^{5y}}$ in the new equation to obtain an expression in terms of $y$ alone:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}&=5e^{5y}\cdot\blueD{\dfrac{dy}{dx}}
\\\\
&=5e^{5y}\cdot\blueD{e^{5y}}
\\\\
&=5e^{10y}
\end{align}$
